WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.600 --> 00:00:05.920
What if a 3D printed part looks completely normal, but is actually

2
00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.320
dangerously weak? In modern manufacturing, that's not

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:15.280
science fiction. It's a real cyber security risk. Today's industrial 3D

4
00:00:15.280 --> 00:00:20.320
printers are connected systems. They can be monitored remotely, controlled

5
00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:26.080
remotely, and unfortunately attacked remotely, and an attacker doesn't need

6
00:00:26.080 --> 00:00:30.400
to break the machine. An attacker can simply tweak process parameters like

7
00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.559
temperature or speed to quietly weaken a part from the inside. The problem is

8
00:00:36.559 --> 00:00:41.680
traditional inspection methods won't always catch this. A part may look fine,

9
00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:47.520
but structurally it could fail. In this work, we ask a simple question. Can we

10
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.440
detect these attacks while a part is being printed? To do this, we used

11
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:55.680
external sensors measuring temperature, vibration, and air quality to monitor

12
00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.840
the printer in real time. Then, we trained a machine learning model called

13
00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:04.640
an autoenccoder to learn what normal printing behavior looks like. When

14
00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:10.080
something unusual happens, the model flags it as an anomaly. We tested this

15
00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:14.799
by intentionally modifying the printing parameters of a laserbased 3D printer

16
00:01:14.799 --> 00:01:19.680
and producing tensile test specimens along with an artifact measurement part.

17
00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:24.080
The tensile specimens were evaluated using a tensile testing machine, while

18
00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:28.640
the artifact parts were scanned with a highresolution 3D scanner to detect

19
00:01:28.640 --> 00:01:35.360
dimensional deviations. The results were striking. Some parts lost over 50% of

20
00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:39.759
their strength, yet still appeared nearly identical under high resolution

21
00:01:39.759 --> 00:01:44.799
scanning. Visually, they passed inspection. However, the sensor data

22
00:01:44.799 --> 00:01:48.880
told a different story. Our model successfully detected many of these

23
00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.600
hidden deviations, identifying when process behavior no longer aligned with

24
00:01:53.600 --> 00:01:58.240
normal operating conditions. This demonstrates that cyber security in

25
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.640
manufacturing is not only about protecting data. It is about protecting

26
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:07.360
the physical integrity of what we produce. By combining sensor data with

27
00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:11.920
machine learning, we can move towards smarter systems that monitor themselves

28
00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:16.959
in real time and detect issues before failures propagate across entire

29
00:02:16.959 --> 00:02:22.080
production batches. As manufacturing becomes more connected, solutions like

30
00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.360
this can play a key role in ensuring not just surface level accuracy, but

31
00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.920
consistent internal quality throughout what we print.

